  "full_response": "A B-tree index is a commonly used data structure in relational databases to efficiently store, retrieve, and manage information. It allows quick access to data rows by maintaining a balanced tree structure that enhances data retrieval speeds. Here's a breakdown of how a B-tree index works:\n\n1. **Structure**:\n   - **Tree Nodes**: The B-tree consists of nodes, with the root node at the top, internal nodes (non-leaf) in the middle, and leaf nodes at the bottom. Each node contains keys and pointers.\n   - **Keys**: These are the values that help in data retrieval and are used to sort records. The keys within a node are ordered.\n   - **Pointers**: These are references to other nodes. In non-leaf nodes, pointers direct the search process towards the appropriate subtree. In leaf nodes, pointers usually point to actual data records or another level of detail.\n\n2. **Balanced Tree**:\n   - A B-tree is balanced, meaning that all leaf nodes are at the same depth. This balance ensures efficient search operations, as the path from the root to any leaf node is of consistent length.\n\n3. **Node Capacity**:\n   - Each node has a minimum and maximum number of keys, defined by the order of the B-tree. Typically, every node except the root must have at least half the maximum number of keys (also known as being half-full), and the root must have at least one key.\n\n4. **Insertion**:\n   - When inserting a new key, the algorithm checks the appropriate node for insertion. If the node has room, the key is inserted in order.\n   - If the node is full, a split occurs, where the node is divided into two nodes and the middle key is promoted to the parent node. This might cause a chain of splits if the parent is also full.\n\n5. **Deletion**:\n   - Deleting a key involves removing it from the node. If this results in an underflow (having less than the minimum number of keys), the database will attempt to borrow a key from a sibling node. If borrowing isn’t possible, it might merge nodes.\n   - The algorithm ensures that the tree remains balanced even after deletions.\n\n6. **Search**:\n   - Searching involves traversing the tree from the root node, following pointers through nodes until the desired key is found in a leaf node.\n   - Due to the ordered nature of the keys within each node, the search is efficient, typically with a logarithmic time complexity depending",
